      evergreen: false,
      title: "The Bank raised rates. That is not a simple signal for sterling gold.",
      dek: "The Bank says policy is not on a pre-set path, and members\u2019 minutes entries disagree about the next move.",
      description: "The Bank of England\u2019s November 2026 vote, the futures window behind it, and why that is not a path for sterling gold. Not investment advice.",
      checked: "5 November 2026, 21:57 UK (GMT)",
      asOf: "Checked against the Bank of England\u2019s 5 November 2026 Monetary Policy Summary, minutes and Monetary Policy Report at 21:57 UK (GMT) on 5 November 2026. This note is not a live price feed. Revising it later would mean reading the documents again, not a new market quote. The futures averages cover the 15 UK working days ending 27 October 2026. September 2026 CPI is an outturn. Figures for 2027 are the Bank\u2019s November projections.",
      sources: [
        { name: "November 2026 Monetary Policy Summary and Minutes", url: "https://www.bankofengland.co.uk/monetary-policy-summary-and-minutes/2026/november-2026", note: "Summary, including \u201cnot on a pre-set path\u201d and the next decision date, 17 December 2026; minutes paragraphs 3, 4, 16 and 19\u201322; the entries for Bailey, Breeden, Dhingra, Greene, Lombardelli, Pill, Ramsden, Taylor and Mann. Checked 5 November 2026, 21:57 UK (GMT)." },
        { name: "November 2026 Monetary Policy Report", url: "https://www.bankofengland.co.uk/monetary-policy-report/2026/november-2026", note: "Chart 1.1; indirect effects around Chart 1.3; section 1.3; Box A; Box E; Section 3 and Table 3.A. Checked 5 November 2026, 21:57 UK (GMT)." }
      ],
      blocks: [
        { h: null, ps: [
          "At the meeting ending 4 November 2026 the Committee voted 8\u20131 to raise Bank Rate by 0.25 percentage points, to 4%. Catherine L Mann voted for an increase of 0.5 percentage points, to 4.25%. The <a href=\"https://www.bankofengland.co.uk/monetary-policy-summary-and-minutes/2026/november-2026\">summary and minutes</a>, published on 5 November 2026, record that vote in the summary and in paragraphs 19\u201322. The summary also says monetary policy is not on a pre-set path. The next minutes, for the meeting ending 16 December, are due on 17 December 2026.",
          "A higher Bank Rate is not, by itself, a signal for gold priced in pounds. The minutes do put a scale on the energy move, and the entries under the vote do not agree what should happen next."
        ] },
        { h: "The scale is a futures average, and the curve slopes down", ps: [
          "Minutes paragraph 3 states the comparison the Committee was using. The front-month Brent crude oil futures contract and the UK front-month natural gas futures contract averaged $108 per barrel and 216 pence per therm, respectively, over the 15 UK working days ending 27 October. Those averages were 38% and 81% higher than at the time of the July Report, and 5% and 27% above that Report\u2019s adverse-scenario assumptions for 2026 Q4 futures prices. Respectively, the first figure in each pair is Brent and the second is UK gas.",
          "Paragraph 4 says the direct energy contribution to inflation is then expected to fall back, using conditioning assumptions based on futures curves that were downward sloping. The Report\u2019s oil chart uses forward prices averaged over the 15 UK working days to 27 October. This is not a spot price on 4 or 5 November. Paragraph 3 says spot and futures prices had risen; it does not publish a separate spot level in that sentence.",
          "CPI has an outturn and a projection. The summary says UK CPI inflation increased to 3.5% in September. Chart 1.1 dates that outturn to September 2026 and shows a projection of around 5% in 2027 Q2. The Report says inflation is projected to average 4.9% in 2027 Q2. Mann\u2019s entry cites projected CPI of 4.9% by mid-2027 and says that projection is conditioned on a significant decline in energy prices. None of the 2027 figures is an outcome."
        ] },
        { h: "What is limited, and what is only delayed", ps: [
          "The summary says there remains limited evidence of knock-on effects on other prices and wages, and that pipeline pressure plus stronger-than-expected activity warranted the increase. Box A says there continues to be little evidence of second-round effects in the data, that this does not rule out stronger effects later, and that the risk has increased. It also says lower margins continue to be the most commonly cited response by firms to the energy shock. An initial estimate of the average 2027 pay settlement should, Box A says, be available in time for the December meeting.",
          "Around Chart 1.3 the Report says firms\u2019 electricity and gas costs have not yet risen significantly. Contacts of the Bank\u2019s Agents suggest that this may be partly explained by greater use of fixed-price contracts and hedging arrangements. Where firms have faced cost increases, the DMP survey suggests they have absorbed some of the rise by accepting lower margins, and the Report points that margins clause to Box A. Bailey\u2019s entry describes the same mechanisms, then says: \u201cTo varying degrees, these approaches can only be temporary.\u201d"
        ] },
        { h: "The vote was shared. The next step was not", ps: [
          "Paragraph 19 says eight members preferred the 0.25 percentage point increase, and that they still differed on how far disinflation had gone, how much slack remained, and how restrictive policy already was. That is a range of views about the present. It is not a published range of future Bank Rate.",
          "Four of those entries look toward a further increase. Bailey writes that, without news which softens the risks to inflationary pressures, he can see the argument for a further increase. Greene writes that additional hikes will likely be required, though later moves depend on pay, pricing behaviour and slack. Lombardelli writes that, in the absence of weaker inflation pressures, there is likely to be a case for a further increase in the near term. Pill writes that, of itself, \u201ca single 25-basis point hike is unlikely to be sufficient\u201d.",
          "Two of the 0.25 percentage point entries set a higher bar for anything beyond this step. Breeden writes that, for now, she judges the restrictiveness of financial conditions sufficient. Dhingra writes that the 0.25 percentage point rise \u201cprovides additional insurance\u201d, and that holding Bank Rate higher for an extended period would need clearer evidence of pricing power and of wage and price pressures reinforcing each other. Taylor, who also voted for 0.25 percentage points, writes that \u201cthis is not the start of a hiking cycle\u201d and that the step is \u201ca risk-management \u2018one-for-now\u2019 insurance hike\u201d. He adds: \u201cThe next move could be up, down, or sideways.\u201d Ramsden writes that the increase is necessary now, and that he will judge the evidence at each meeting. Mann had already voted for 4.25%. The 8\u20131 figure counts this proposition. It does not count who expects another one."
        ] },
        { h: "The curve is not a promise", ps: [
          "Section 1.3 says that, on the 15-day average to 27 October, the market curve rises to around 4.9% at the end of 2027 and then falls to around 4.7% by late 2029. Chart 1.15\u2019s note dates those November curves to the 15 UK working days to 27 October 2026. The same section says the curve contains both expected Bank Rate and risk premia. The median in the November Market Participants Survey had Bank Rate at 4% in November, 4.25% in February, and 3.5% two and three years ahead. The Report says the difference between the market curve and that path suggests that risk premia continue to contribute to the upward slope of the market curve. It does not say the curve will be delivered.",
          "The same section records that, in September, the ECB raised its deposit facility rate by 25 basis points to 2.50%, and the Federal Reserve raised the federal funds target range by 25 basis points to 3.75%\u20134.00%. The November UK decision was not taken against foreign policy rates that the Report still had unchanged."
        ] },
        { h: "Sterling, gilt yields, and gold are three different questions", ps: [
          "Box E says higher UK rates can lead sterling to appreciate, which can lower import prices and CPI inflation within a year, and can also reduce demand for UK exports. It then says the exchange-rate channel did not contribute materially to fast transmission in 2021\u201323, because other central banks also tightened, which limited changes in interest-rate differentials and exchange rates. Section 1.3\u2019s September ECB and Fed decisions are the Report\u2019s current illustration of that point: the UK was not the only central bank that had been raising rates.",
          "A dollar gold reference price becomes a sterling figure by dividing by the pound\u2019s dollar price at the same time. If dollar gold were unchanged, a stronger pound would lower that sterling figure and a weaker pound would raise it. If dollar gold also moved, both changes would count. The minutes do not measure either move, and they do not give a gilt yield. Gilt yields can show expected rates and risk premia. They are not the same object as a real yield, and they are not the sterling price of gold. The Bank\u2019s own alternatives for energy prices, demand and second-round effects are in Section 3 and Table 3.A. The table below is only a way to watch the three cases in minutes paragraph 16."
        ] },
        { h: "What would count against each case", ps: [
          "<div class=\"c101h-watch\"><table><caption>Paragraph 16\u2019s three cases. Not a forecast, and not a ranking.</caption><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Case</th><th scope=\"col\">What would count against it</th><th scope=\"col\">What it can and cannot say for gold</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td data-h=\"Case\">Energy prices rise above the futures path and stay there; second-round effects build.</td><td data-h=\"What would count against it\">Front-month Brent and UK gas futures fall back toward the downward-sloping curve instead of staying above it, or inflation expectations do not follow headline inflation.</td><td data-h=\"What it can and cannot say for gold\">Higher energy costs can lift near-term inflation. Whether the real return on cash rises or falls depends on whether Bank Rate and gilt yields move by more or less than expected inflation. Neither sets the sterling price of gold.</td></tr><tr><td data-h=\"Case\">Central projection: wholesale oil and gas follow the futures curves averaged over the 15 working days to 27 October, which slope downward.</td><td data-h=\"What would count against it\">Front-month prices stay at or above the late-October averages instead of easing along the curve.</td><td data-h=\"What it can and cannot say for gold\">Easier expected policy, or lower risk premia, would be a change in the curve in section 1.3. A change in sterling changes the pound value of an unchanged dollar gold price only as arithmetic. The minutes give neither size nor sign.</td></tr><tr><td data-h=\"Case\">Weaker demand: growth gives way and spare capacity limits second-round effects.</td><td data-h=\"What would count against it\">Activity stays resilient, as the summary says it recently has, the labour market keeps stabilising (Box C), and pay starts to respond.</td><td data-h=\"What it can and cannot say for gold\">More slack would, on the Bank\u2019s account, lean against second-round effects. Gold in pounds would still depend on dollar gold and the exchange rate, which this case does not decide.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>",
          "The 8\u20131 vote, the futures average and the disagreement about the next step are three statements. The next published decision is 17 December 2026, which is also when Box A says an initial estimate of 2027 pay settlements should be available. That date is a reason to look again at the Bank\u2019s documents. It is not a quote for gold, gilts or sterling.",
          "Correction, 5 November 2026: An earlier version of this note attributed the fixed-price contracts and hedging point to Box A. That point is in the Report\u2019s discussion around Chart 1.3. Box A covers margins, saying lower margins continue to be the most commonly cited response by firms to the energy shock."
        ] }
      ]
